WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.580
Recovery is where many MFA designs become fragile.

2
00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:14.280
Users lose devices, replace phones, forget backup codes, leave organizations, change roles, or need emergency access.

3
00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:19.420
The recovery process must balance security, availability, and support reality.

4
00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:23.120
If recovery is too weak, it undermines MFA.

5
00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:28.980
If recovery is impossible, legitimate users and operations may be locked out at critical moments.

6
00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:31.500
Backup code handling should be deliberate.

7
00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:41.680
Backup codes should be difficult to guess, limited in use, stored carefully by the user, protected by the application, and retired after use where appropriate.

8
00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:47.060
MFA reset should require suitable proof and should generate audit evidence.

9
00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:55.480
Help desk procedures need identity verification, approval boundaries, logging, and escalation paths, especially for privileged accounts.

10
00:00:55.760 --> 00:01:00.720
Lifecycle governance connects MFA to joiner, mover, and leaver processes.

11
00:01:00.820 --> 00:01:04.420
New users may need enrollment before accessing protected resources.

12
00:01:04.520 --> 00:01:08.040
Users who change roles may need different factor requirements.

13
00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:13.180
Departing users should lose access through account disablement and session controls.

14
00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:16.720
Dormant accounts and stale factors should be reviewed.

15
00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:22.580
Factor expiration or replacement may be necessary when authenticator risk changes or when devices are lost.

16
00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:26.300
Emergency access should be designed before it is needed.

17
00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:35.520
Break-glass accounts, privileged recovery procedures, and incident response overrides require tight controls, monitoring, and after-action review.

18
00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:40.180
User notifications should accompany factor changes and resets when appropriate.

19
00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:49.080
Recovery should not be treated as a side door; it is part of the main identity lifecycle and must be protected as carefully as normal MFA verification.
